# Project setup

## Complete the hosting configuration

This project targets Kinsta with PHP 8.5 and Deployer. Local development starts on
`develop`; the live deployment and Renovate target branch is `main`.

Before enabling automation, complete these project-specific values:

| File | Values to replace |
| --- | --- |
| `deploy.php` | GitLab repository SSH URL, `REPLACE_SSH_USER`, `ssh.example.com` and SSH port `0` |
| `config/environments/development.php` | Matching SSH user, hostname and port; absolute remote project path `/path/to/public/current` |
| `autoupdate.json` | Replace `https://example.com` with the live origin, retaining `/wp-json/orbit/up` |

Use the actual Kinsta SSH connection details, not the public website hostname.
The sync path points to the active Deployer project under `public/current`, not
the `web` directory or an individual numbered release. Deployer itself inherits
its deployment root and shared paths from the Kinsta recipe.

Create the GitLab project, configure its remote and establish `main` before
enabling the update workflow. Complete the team's GitLab Renovate onboarding
and CI credential setup. Provision the Kinsta deployment layout and webroot
using the team's current first-deployment procedure before the first deployment.
These files do not provision hosting, credentials or GitLab settings.

## Local onboarding after cloning

Requirements: PHP 8.5, Composer 2.10+, WP-CLI with the eighteen73 commands,
and Node/npm versions matching `package.json`.

1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and supply local database credentials, local URLs,
   environment type and authentication salts. Never commit `.env`.
2. Run `composer install` and `npm ci` using the committed lockfiles.
3. Configure your local web server to serve `web`, with HTTPS matching `.env`.
4. With an accessible remote site and SSH access, initialise the local database
   using `wp eighteen73 first-sync`. Read the command documentation first.
5. Run `npm run build`, or `npm run start` while developing the theme.

Project functionality, including post types and taxonomies, belongs in
`web/app/mu-plugins/nebula-core`; presentation belongs in
`web/app/themes/pulsar`. Use `npm run create:post-type`, `npm run create:taxonomy`
and `npm run create:block` for the supplied generators.

## Database, uploads and email

The SSH settings in `config/environments/development.php` are shared by `sync`
and `first-sync`. Personal `.env` settings can override the shared sync settings.

- `wp eighteen73 sync`: apply configured plugin overrides only.
- `wp eighteen73 sync --database`: replace the local database from the remote site.
- `wp eighteen73 sync --uploads`: download remote uploads.
- `wp eighteen73 sync --database --uploads`: do both.

Database sync overwrites local data. See the [sync documentation](https://docs.eighteen73.co.uk/wordpress/wp-cli/command/sync.html)
and [first-sync documentation](https://docs.eighteen73.co.uk/wordpress/wp-cli/command/first-sync.html).
Optional plugin overrides are commented out in the development configuration;
enable them to suit the project.

Development SMTP points to a local mail catcher. Check `SMTP_PORT` in
`config/environments/development.php` matches the catcher on your machine.
Configure production delivery separately.

## Persistent object caching

Docket Cache is installed and activated on the initial site. Its settings
live in `config/includes/docket-cache.php`. Object caching is enabled locally and
on hosting; additional query tweaks, WooCommerce tweaks, preloading and Cronbot
are disabled. Upstream cache size/expiry defaults remain in place.

Composer install/update runs `scripts/install-docket-cache.php` to generate the
drop-in matching the installed plugin. Run Composer with scripts enabled. The
installer does not need WordPress, a database or a running web server, and refuses
to replace another backend's drop-in. Generated files are not committed.

Cache files (`web/app/cache/docket-cache`) and runtime data
(`web/app/docket-cache-data`) stay local to each release. Do not add these paths
or `web/app/object-cache.php` to Deployer's shared lists. New releases start with
fresh cache storage. Flush the restored release's cache after a rollback.

Use `wp cache status` to inspect Docket and `wp cache flush` to flush it.
Database sync already invokes a cache flush. Docket can report an error when
flushing an empty cache. Its CLI-to-web OPcache invalidation uses an authenticated
HTTP request to `/wp-json/docket-cache/v1/opcache-invalidate`; verify web requests
observe CLI changes during hosting setup, including when access controls exist.
The bridge needs runtime data created by the web application and loopback access.

When moving to SQLite or Redis, remove the Docket Composer hooks and configuration,
disable/remove its drop-in and deactivate the plugin before enabling the new
backend. Remove its Composer dependency and installer script as part of that
change so subsequent deployments cannot reinstall the Docket drop-in.

## Deployment and updates

- Manual deployment: `vendor/bin/dep deploy live`, once hosting is configured.
- Deployer builds theme assets; generated theme build output is not committed.
- Renovate inherits the agency preset in `renovate.json`.
- `autoupdate.json` enables automatic patch and minor merging into `main`.
- GitLab CI includes the shared dependency-update and patch-deploy components.
  Remove the patch-deploy include if another pipeline already deploys on merge
  to `main`, to avoid duplicate deployments.
- The patch-deploy workflow uses the live Orbit health endpoint for its
  post-deployment check and rollback behaviour.

PHP is configured in `composer.json` (`require.php: ~8.5.0` and
`config.platform.php: 8.5`) and both CI component inputs. Keep these aligned with
Kinsta when changing PHP versions, and refresh the Composer lockfile.

Composer uses the WP Sec Adv repository and permits advisory-affected versions
during dependency resolution. `composer audit --lock` still reports advisories
and exits non-zero when findings exist; review those findings during maintenance.

For local checks use `composer run lint` and `npm run lint`; use `npm run build`
to verify the production asset build.
